00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.949
confusing moment that we're living
through. So now I get to share with you

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:22.580
guys one of my favorite episodes so far.
Here we go.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:27.900
By now you've probably heard of Nadia
the tiger but if not allow me to

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:28.940
introduce you.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:32.640
A tiger at a zoo in New
York has tested positive for coronavirus

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.616
Researchers think that she caught it
from a human zookeeper.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.360
At the zoo four

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:42.140
year old Nadia her sister Azul, two amur
Tigers and three lions all developed dry

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:42.620
coughs.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:46.829
Don't worry. Nadia's doing a lot
better now but her illness highlighted

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:51.510
something about the virus itself it's
zoonotic. That means it can transmit

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.440
between humans and animals.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.309
Now the bond
between a Pomeranian and its owner may

00:00:56.309 --> 00:00:58.080
have taken a serious turn.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.239
The study
showed Winston the family's fun-loving

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:02.660
pug contracted COVID-19.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.610
According to the
CDC and the US Department of Agriculture

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.932
two cats in separate parts of New York
tested positive.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.240
All of this has me wondering.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.780
Which animals can catch
coronavirus? Should I be worried about my

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.240
pet? I'm Cleo Abram and this is answered
by Vox.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.040
Well for most animals that we've

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:27.920
seen that can be infected by COVID-19,
they don't have very serious symptoms .

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.240
That's Dr. William Karesh. 
He's a wildlife veterinarian and an

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:33.120
expert on animals and pandemics.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.560
I have a dog. Should I be worried about my dog?

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.061
No. You should not be worried about your dog.

00:01:39.061 --> 00:01:41.939
Dogs have been shown in rare cases to

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:45.624
pick up the virus but the virus doesn't
grow very well in dogs.

00:01:45.624 --> 00:01:46.560
People found out

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.479
that dogs have it just out of curiosity
because the people in the homes were

00:01:51.479 --> 00:01:55.170
very sick and they thought well let's
just test the dogs and see if they might

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.560
have it. And they picked up the virus but
the dogs weren't sick.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:02.800
Cats seem to be more susceptible and cats can actually
infect other cats

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.240
but they don't get very sick.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.179
While it is possible for our
pets to get very mild cases of COVID-19

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:14.030
the CDC says "The risk
of animals spreading COVID-19 to people

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:15.880
is considered to be low."

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.920
So it seems like
we can take our pets getting sick or

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.944
getting us sick off our list of worries.

00:02:20.944 --> 00:02:24.319
But it's clear that COVID-19 can

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:28.099
transmit between humans and animals so
researchers are trying to figure out

00:02:28.099 --> 00:02:31.200
which animals are most susceptible.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.690
Once the virus breaks into an animal's body

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:37.640
it needs to fit itself inside a special
receptor on the target cell called ACE2

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.140
the virus and receptor act kind of like
a lock and key.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:46.340
The more easily the SARS-CoV2 virus latches on to a
species ACE2 receptor,

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:49.640
the more likely that animal is to become infected.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.780
Each species' ACE2 receptor is a little bit different,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.800
so not all animals get
infected equally.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:02.480
The virus is more likely to bind in humans, camels, cats,
pangolins and bats

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.620
but less likely in rats, mice, chickens and guinea pigs.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.500
So this is the first part of the story

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:13.099
which which animals can it bind to,  but
it's not the whole story because we know

00:03:13.099 --> 00:03:18.440
that pigs actually don't have a
productive infection even though they

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:20.060
can receive the virus.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.700
Which is why so
many animals are susceptible to the

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.780
virus, but it doesn't make them sick.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.030
We don't want to jump to too many

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:31.520
conclusions but it's a beautiful start
to a way to look at susceptibility.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:35.450
The ACE2 receptor is really only an
indicator of whether or not an animal

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:39.680
can become infected with coronavirus. It
doesn't tell us anything about how

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:42.700
coronavirus can spread between humans
and animals.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.300
I've heard people talk about bats, about pangolins,

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.480
about wet markets,

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.320
So I'm wondering what do we know for sure

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.660
about how COVID-19 got from animals to
humans?

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:59.320
Well right now we don't know what the original animal source was.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:01.540
We know that there's many viruses

00:04:01.549 --> 00:04:06.139
very similar to COVID-19 like all it's
kissing cousins and his brothers and

00:04:06.139 --> 00:04:09.300
sisters.We find those in bats.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.910
Many of
the viruses that make us sick originally

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:11.410
came from the animal kingdom. The common
cold originated in camels.

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:16.800
Many strains of flu come from pigs and birds.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.859
HIV
transferred to humans from a chimpanzee.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:25.520
Ebola, SARS, Marburg, Nipah, and COVID-19
have all been linked to bats.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:30.760
Is there a specific reason why
these diseases come from bats as opposed

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.320
to other animals?

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.510
There's over 1,400 types of bats so

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:39.470
there are a lot of viruses for one
thing. And then another is genetically,

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.140
we're not so distantly related to bats.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.720
They're closer on the evolutionary tree,

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:49.860
they're closer to people than a lot of
other animals are.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:54.940
So the viruses of course then, it's easier to share viruses
with things you're related to like

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:59.820
humans and gorillas can share a lot more
viruses than anybody else.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:06.640
Why is it though that when a virus like SARS-CoV2
jumps from animals to people it's so

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:10.620
deadly to us whereas it's less so to the
original host animal?

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:17.060
All of us have viruses and bacteria living on us and we
have grown used to them and over the

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:21.530
millennia we've actually evolved to use
them when we spread them among species

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:23.660
they have very different reactions.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:29.040
Over the last hundred years, 
the number of zoonotic diseases in people has been
increasing.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:30.590
On average a new infectious

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:36.020
disease emerges in humans every four
months and 75% of them come from animals.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:40.610
Zoonotic diseases have been around as
long as there have been people and

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:45.590
animals together. What's new is what we
call these new emerging infectious

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:50.480
diseases and that's a new virus like
COVID-19. Those are becoming more and

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:55.370
more common. We have more exposure to wildlife as we encroach into wild areas

00:05:55.370 --> 00:06:00.560
as we disturb habitats. They spread
faster because of air travel and trade

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.080
so we live in a new world.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.510
For now,  we don't need to worry too much about the

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:08.180
animals in our lives getting sick from
COVID-19.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:11.920
But we should be worried about health and our relationship with the
environment.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:13.000
We need to detect these

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.760
things right away; not wait until they
turn into a pandemic

